              "content": {
                "id": "p33-q11",
                "type": "question",
                "label": "11",
                "prompt": "Show that $2^{2m+3}+4^m$ is a multiple of 3 for all positive integers $m$.",
                "answerSpaceMm": 38,
                "answer": {
                  "short": "$2^{2m+3}+4^m=9\\times2^{2m}=3(3\\times2^{2m})$. Since $3\\times2^{2m}$ is an integer, the expression is a multiple of 3.",
                  "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2^{2m+3}+4^m&=8\\times2^{2m}+2^{2m}\\\\&=(8+1)2^{2m}\\\\&=9\\times2^{2m}\\\\&=3(3\\times2^{2m})\\end{align*}$$\nFor every positive integer $m$, $3\\times2^{2m}$ is an integer. Therefore the expression is a multiple of 3.",
                  "provenance": {
                    "short": "authored",
                    "worked": "authored"
                  }
                }
              },
              "sourceReview": {
                "teachingContext": [
